# Interview Transcript with Josephine

**Q: What information about courses do you think is the most important?**  
A: To me, if I want to take a course, what I will be learning, like the subtopics, is relevant for me to know before taking a course.  

---

**Q: Would you find a custom plan for your entire degree useful? Would you use it?**  
A: It can be useful, especially if you’re only starting, but I feel like as you go, changes can be made according to your circumstances or situations.  

---

**Q: If you could redesign UQ's public programs and courses page, would you? How?**  
A: For sure, make it less of a text dump, more visual, and with a better search.  

---

**Q: What do you find is the most annoying feature of the course profile?**  
A: The fact that everything is buried. Show me the assessment table first.  

---

**Q: If you take a dual degree, have you seen your planner? What did you like about it? What didn’t you like about it?**  
A: I mean, it’s there but not flexible. It’s hard to experiment with swaps.  

---

**Q: Do you use Allocate+’s timetable? What don’t you like about it? What do you like about it?**  
A: It’s convenient but clunky. Swaps are a bit of a pain, and the UI is ancient.  

---

**Q: What UQ website has your favourite UI design? What has your least favourite design?**  
A: For the best, I would say Program and Courses, no doubt, because it’s very informative. The worst is maybe SI-net because it’s just simple and old.  

---

**Q: Would you find it useful if our website offered alternative courses to take if the course you were going to take ends up full or for some other reason you can no longer take it?**  
A: I personally find it useful to be offered further options that I can take.  

---

**Q: Do you think being able to plan your entire degree out in a visual timeline will reduce confusion compared to just the current list of courses?**  
A: Honestly, I would find it useful because I’m a visual person and I use planners, especially reminders a lot, because I can be forgetful at times.  

---

**Q: Have you seen a student talk about your specific degree? Do you think if you did it would make course selection less overwhelming?**  
A: I personally haven’t heard other students talking about my specific degree, so hearing about courses from students that know would be helpful for me.  

---

**Q: Do you think having a space to chat with other students who are doing the same course or have done the same course in the past will help?**  
A: Certainly, because that will improve my knowledge or just common information that I should know.  
